theory.intro:
An **arc** is part of the circumference of a circle. Its length $l$ is the radius $r$ multiplied by the central angle $\theta$, measured in radians. Convert any angle given in degrees before using the formula.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (3,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (-1.5,2.598);
\draw (O) circle (3);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (P) node[midway, below] {$r$};
\draw[thick] (O) -- (Q) node[midway, above left] {$r$};
\draw[thick, blue] (P) arc (0:120:3) node[midway, above right] {$l$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.8cm, angle eccentricity=1.6] {angle=P--O--Q};
\node[below left] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The minor arc $AB$ of a circle of radius $6\text{ cm}$ subtends $\dfrac{\pi}{3}$ radians at the centre $O$. Find the exact length of the major arc $AB$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{arcblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (2,0);
\coordinate (B) at (60:2);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\draw (A)--(O)--(B);
\draw[arcblue,thick] (B) arc[start angle=60,end angle=360,radius=2];
\draw (0.5,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=60,radius=0.5];
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$};
\path (A) node[right] {$A$};
\path (B) node[above right] {$B$};
\path (1,0) node[below] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\path (30:0.95) node {$\frac{\pi}{3}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

